Subject: [PATCH v2 0/4] Require an interdiff on revised series
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 03:34:42 +0000

Revised series keep reaching the list without an interdiff against
their previous version, so the reviewer has to reread every patch to
find what changed. Pat asked that the interdiff be computed
deterministically from the previous version rather than inferred.

Patch 1 adds series-rebuild.sh. Given the Message-ID of a series'
cover letter or only patch, it applies the posted patches onto their
base-commit, or onto another commit with -o, and prints the range.
The previous version is therefore what reached the list, whatever
became of its commits in anyone's tree. Rebuilding all six signoz
versions this way gives trees identical to the commits they were
formatted from.

Patch 2 makes format-patch.sh refuse -v N for N > 1 without
--previous=<message-id>. It rebuilds that version onto the new
--base and passes git --interdiff against it, so a rebase does not
bury the changes under everything that landed between the bases.
If the previous version no longer applies to the new base, it warns
and gives a range-diff instead.

Patch 3 states the rule in src/lab-process-mail/prompt.md and the
mail workflow guide. Patch 4 does the same for the freebsd-port
skill, whose patches are formatted with plain git in a ports tree,
which is how the signoz revisions escaped the check.

I tested series-rebuild.sh on single patches and covers, on lab and
ports trees, and its refusals: a non-cover patch of a series, a
missing base, a patch that does not apply. format-patch.sh refuses
-v 2 without --previous, and formatted this series with it.

Changes in v2:
- Rebuild the previous version from the list instead of taking its
  head from the leading hash of a Message-ID, as asked in "New patch
  series must have an interdiff" [1].
- Apply it onto the new base so the interdiff shows only the
  revision, with a range-diff fallback.
- Move the instructions from the removed process-email skill to
  src/lab-process-mail/prompt.md, and cover the freebsd-port skill.
- The range-diff below pairs patches poorly: v1's patch 2 edited
  the process-email skill, which is gone from the base, so v1 no
